# Ex ranges typed from normal mode (cmdline.txt *cmdline-ranges*).

=== :N deletes an absolute line number
ref:    cmdline.txt *E1247*
quote:  an absolute line number
given:
  |one
  two
  three
  four
keys:   :3d<CR>
expect:
  one
  two
  |four
mode:   normal

=== :N alone moves the cursor to that line
ref:    motion.txt *:[range]*
quote:  Set the cursor on the last line number in [range].
given:
  |one
    two
  three
keys:   :2<CR>
expect:
  one
  |  two
  three
mode:   normal

=== :. is the cursor line
ref:    cmdline.txt *:.*
quote:  the current line
given:
  one
  t|wo
  three
keys:   :.d<CR>
expect:
  one
  |three
mode:   normal

=== :$ is the last line
ref:    cmdline.txt *:$*
quote:  the last line of the buffer
given:
  |one
  two
  three
keys:   :$d<CR>
expect:
  one
  |two
mode:   normal

=== :% deletes every line
ref:    cmdline.txt *:%*
quote:  equal to 1,$ (the entire buffer)
given:
  one
  |two
  three
keys:   :%d<CR>
expect: |
mode:   normal

=== :N,M deletes a range and reports the lines
ref:    options.txt *'report'*
quote:  When the number of changed lines is more than 'report' a message will be given for most ":" commands.
given:
  |one
  two
  three
  four
  five
keys:   :2,4d<CR>
expect:
  one
  |five
msg:    3 fewer lines
mode:   normal

=== two deleted lines are not reported
ref:    options.txt *'report'*
quote:  When the number of changed lines is more than 'report' a message will be given for most ":" commands.
given:
  |one
  two
  three
  four
keys:   :2,3d<CR>
expect:
  one
  |four
msg:    ""
mode:   normal

=== :'a,'b uses mark lines
ref:    cmdline.txt *:'x*
quote:  the line of the position of mark x
given:
  one
  t|wo
  three
  four
  five
keys:   majjmbgg:'a,'bd<CR>
expect:
  one
  |five
mode:   normal

=== an unset mark in a range is an error
ref:    motion.txt *E20*
quote:  Marks
given:
  |one
  two
keys:   :'q,.d<CR>
expect:
  |one
  two
msg:    E20: Mark not set
mode:   normal

=== :/pat/ is the next matching line
ref:    cmdline.txt *:/*
quote:  the next line where {pattern} matches
given:
  |foo one
  two
  foo three
  four
keys:   :/foo/d<CR>
expect:
  foo one
  two
  |four
mode:   normal

=== :/pat/ skips a match in the cursor line
ref:    cmdline.txt *:?*
quote:  Note: "next line" and "previous line" do not include matches appearing in the current line.
given:
  one
  |foo two
  three
  foo four
keys:   :/foo/d<CR>
expect:
  one
  foo two
  |three
mode:   normal

=== :/pat/ wraps around the end of the buffer
ref:    cmdline.txt *:/*
quote:  the next line where {pattern} matches
given:
  foo one
  two
  |three
keys:   :/foo/d<CR>
expect:
  |two
  three
mode:   normal

=== :?pat? is the previous matching line
ref:    cmdline.txt *:?*
quote:  the previous line where {pattern} matches
given:
  foo one
  foo two
  three
  |four
keys:   :?foo?d<CR>
expect:
  foo one
  |three
  four
mode:   normal

=== :/pat/ without a match is an error
ref:    pattern.txt *E486*
quote:  Pattern not found
given:
  |one
  two
keys:   :/nothere/d<CR>
expect:
  |one
  two
msg:    "E486: Pattern not found: nothere"
mode:   normal

=== :/pat/+1 is the line after the match
ref:    cmdline.txt *:range-pattern*
quote:  the line below the next line containing "that"
given:
  |one
  that
  three
  four
keys:   :/that/+1d<CR>
expect:
  one
  that
  |four
mode:   normal

=== :/pat1//pat2/ searches from the first match
ref:    cmdline.txt *:range-pattern*
quote:  Find line containing "pat2" after line containing "pat1", without moving the cursor.
given:
  |one
  pat2 early
  pat1
  three
  pat2 late
keys:   :/pat1//pat2/d<CR>
expect:
  one
  pat2 early
  pat1
  |three
mode:   normal

=== :\/ is the next line matching the last search
ref:    cmdline.txt *:?*
quote:  the next line where the most recent search pattern matches
given:
  |one
  foo
  three
  foo
keys:   /foo<CR>gg:\/d<CR>
expect:
  one
  |three
  foo
mode:   normal

=== :\? is the previous line matching the last search
ref:    cmdline.txt *:?*
quote:  the previous line where the most recent search pattern matches
given:
  |foo
  two
  foo
  four
keys:   /foo<CR>G:\?d<CR>
expect:
  foo
  two
  |four
mode:   normal

=== :\& is the next line matching the last substitute pattern
ref:    cmdline.txt *:?*
quote:  the next line where the most recent substitute pattern matches
given:
  |bar
  one
  bar
  two
keys:   :s/bar/baz/<CR>:\&d<CR>
expect:
  baz
  one
  |two
mode:   normal

=== :.+N is relative to the cursor line
ref:    cmdline.txt *:range-offset*
quote:  That value is added or subtracted from the preceding line number.
given:
  |one
  two
  three
  four
keys:   :.+2d<CR>
expect:
  one
  two
  |four
mode:   normal

=== :- alone is the line above
ref:    cmdline.txt *:range-offset*
quote:  If there is nothing before the '+' or '-', for the first line number in [range] the current line is used as the relative starting point.
given:
  one
  two
  |three
keys:   :-d<CR>
expect:
  one
  |three
mode:   normal

=== :++ counts each plus as one
ref:    cmdline.txt *:range-offset*
quote:  If the number is omitted, +1 is used for each '+' and -1 for each '-' so, e.g., 'x++ and 'x+2 are synonymous.
given:
  |one
  two
  three
  four
keys:   :++d<CR>
expect:
  one
  two
  |four
mode:   normal

=== :-,. is the line before to the cursor line
ref:    cmdline.txt *:range-offset*
quote:  So, -,. means, "the line before the current line to the current line".
given:
  one
  two
  |three
  four
keys:   :-,.d<CR>
expect:
  one
  |four
mode:   normal

=== :$-1 counts back from the last line
ref:    cmdline.txt *:range-offset*
quote:  That value is added or subtracted from the preceding line number.
given:
  |one
  two
  three
  four
keys:   :$-1d<CR>
expect:
  one
  two
  |four
mode:   normal

=== :'a+1 is the line after a mark
ref:    cmdline.txt *:range-offset*
quote:  So, for example, 'x+2 is two lines after the line containing mark x.
given:
  |one
  two
  three
  four
keys:   ma:'a+2d<CR>
expect:
  one
  two
  |four
mode:   normal

=== a comma searches the second address from the cursor
ref:    cmdline.txt *:,*
quote:  from line 4 till match with "this line" after the cursor line.
given:
  a x
  b
  c
  |d x
  e
  f x
keys:   :2,/x/d<CR>
expect:
  |a x
msg:    5 fewer lines
mode:   normal

=== a semicolon searches the second address from the first
ref:    cmdline.txt *:;*
quote:  When separated with ';' the cursor position will be set to that line before interpreting the next line specifier.
given:
  a x
  b
  c
  |d x
  e
  f x
keys:   :2;/x/d<CR>
expect:
  a x
  |e
  f x
msg:    3 fewer lines
mode:   normal

=== a semicolon makes an offset relative to the first address
ref:    cmdline.txt *:;*
quote:  When separated with ';' the cursor position will be set to that line before interpreting the next line specifier.
given:
  |one
  two
  three
  four
  five
keys:   :3;+1d<CR>
expect:
  one
  two
  |five
mode:   normal

=== 0;/pat/ also matches in the first line
ref:    cmdline.txt *:range-pattern*
quote:  the first line containing "that", also matches in the first line.
given:
  that
  two
  |three
  that
keys:   :0;/that/d<CR>
expect:
  |two
  three
  that
mode:   normal

=== 1;/pat/ skips the first line
ref:    cmdline.txt *:range-pattern*
quote:  the first line after line 1 containing "that"
given:
  that
  two
  |three
  that
  five
keys:   :1;/that/d<CR>
expect:
  |five
msg:    4 fewer lines
mode:   normal

=== extra comma-separated addresses drop the leftmost
ref:    cmdline.txt *:,*
quote:  If more line specifiers are given than required for the command, when comma separated, the leftmost one(s) will be ignored
given:
  |one
  two
  three
  four
keys:   :1,2,3d<CR>
expect:
  one
  |four
mode:   normal

=== extra semicolon-separated addresses are summed
ref:    cmdline.txt *:,*
quote:  When semicolon separated, the leftmost specifier to the penultimate one are summed
given:
  |one
  two
  three
  four
  five
keys:   :1;+1;+1d<CR>
expect:
  one
  |four
  five
mode:   normal

=== a count after the command counts from the last address
ref:    cmdline.txt *:range-pattern*
quote:  This count is used as the number of lines to be used, starting with the line given in the last line specifier (the default is the cursor line).
given:
  |one
  two
  three
  four
  five
keys:   :2d 3<CR>
expect:
  one
  |five
mode:   normal

=== a count after a range starts at the range's last line
ref:    cmdline.txt *:range-pattern*
quote:  This count is used as the number of lines to be used, starting with the line given in the last line specifier (the default is the cursor line).
given:
  |one
  two
  three
  four
  five
keys:   :1,2d 2<CR>
expect:
  one
  |four
  five
mode:   normal

=== a count before : becomes .,.+(count-1)
ref:    cmdline.txt *N:*
quote:  The "count" lines at and after the cursor.
given:
  one
  |two
  three
  four
  five
keys:   3:d<CR>
expect:
  one
  |five
mode:   normal

=== :'<,'> is the last visual area
ref:    cmdline.txt *:'<*
quote:  the first line of the most recently
given:
  one
  t|wo
  three
  four
  five
keys:   vj<Esc>gg:'<,'>d<CR>
expect:
  one
  |four
  five
mode:   normal

=== : in visual mode starts with '<,'>
ref:    cmdline.txt *v_:*
quote:  Starts a command-line with the Visual selected lines as a range.
given:
  one
  t|wo
  three
  four
keys:   Vj:d<CR>
expect:
  one
  |four
mode:   normal

=== :* is the last visual area
ref:    cmdline.txt *:star*
quote:  When Visual mode was already ended, a short way to use the Visual area for a range is `:*`.
given:
  one
  |two
  three
  four
keys:   vj<Esc>G:*d<CR>
expect:
  one
  |four
mode:   normal

=== :0 is taken as line 1 by :d
ref:    cmdline.txt *:range-pattern*
quote:  When using a 0 (zero) this is interpreted as a 1 by most commands.
given:
  one
  |two
  three
keys:   :0d<CR>
expect:
  |two
  three
mode:   normal

=== a line number past the end is an error
ref:    cmdline.txt *E16*
quote:  Some Ex commands accept a line range in front of them.
given:
  |one
  two
keys:   :5d<CR>
expect:
  |one
  two
msg:    "E16: Invalid range"
mode:   normal

=== an offset past the end is an error
ref:    cmdline.txt *E16*
quote:  Some Ex commands accept a line range in front of them.
given:
  one
  |two
keys:   :.+3d<CR>
expect:
  one
  |two
msg:    "E16: Invalid range"
mode:   normal

=== :N past the end moves to the last line
ref:    motion.txt *:[range]*
quote:  Set the cursor on the last line number in [range].
given:
  |one
  two
  three
keys:   :9<CR>
expect:
  one
  two
  |three
mode:   normal

=== a backwards range asks to swap
ref:    cmdline.txt *E493*
quote:  If this is not the case, Vim will ask you if it should swap the line numbers.
given:
  |one
  two
  three
  four
keys:   :3,2d<CR>y
expect:
  one
  |four
mode:   normal

=== a backwards range is not swapped when declined
ref:    cmdline.txt *E493*
quote:  If this is not the case, Vim will ask you if it should swap the line numbers.
given:
  |one
  two
  three
  four
keys:   :3,2d<CR>n
expect:
  |one
  two
  three
  four
mode:   normal

=== :silent swaps a backwards range without asking
ref:    cmdline.txt *E493*
quote:  You can use ":silent" before a command to avoid the question, the range will always be swapped then.
given:
  |one
  two
  three
  four
keys:   :silent 3,2d<CR>
expect:
  one
  |four
mode:   normal
